8 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386226 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f09c0cc7-72c0-5239-9e43-40b952099e41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386227 #edb/ref 1080 "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 13\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386227 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386227 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e74e7e5c-cf0d-59bc-add5-40647a04b0e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386228 #edb/ref 1080 "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386228 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386228 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4564ce1-09d3-5508-b26b-115ede26d625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402568 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae2d41a6-3045-5117-8d5e-3a9ab6755a10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402568 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+3z=7 \\\\ 0x+y+2z=-3 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3z=7 \\\\ y+2z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3z=7\\,⇒\\,x=7-3z\n$$\n\nand from the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny+2z=-3\\,⇒\\,y=-3-2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7-3z \\\\ y=-3-2z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7-3z \\\\ -3-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186402572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402569 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "84f00b54-07f8-565a-95ff-d33549fee4dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402570 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-3-3z \\\\ 7-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402570 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402570 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "547016f8-709b-55ae-9ed1-84a5249a6fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402571 #edb/ref 1080 "No solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402571 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402571 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad3871c3-7636-59b3-aaf5-402cb593106f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402572 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}7-3z \\\\ -3-2z \\\